From 1a1a72a946a9de96dcb1a6a74eace2089576f484 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cpkt9762 <cpkt9762@163.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 16:39:05 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] feat(store): redact credential-shaped text before persisting

Forward-port of fork patch 0005 onto the restructured upstream write path.

Upstream's only write-path filter is still stripPrivateTags, a bare
`(?is)<private>.*?</private>` regex that depends on the agent having
wrapped the secret in tags. It does not. The corpus migrated into this
store carried 11 unique credentials across 43 rows -- 2 GitHub PATs and 7
sk- API keys among them -- several inside observations whose whole subject
was reporting that a credential had leaked.

sanitizeForStorage layers seven vendor-anchored patterns on top of the tag
strip. Ordinary prose is left alone, which the regression tests pin
explicitly: "we use sk-learn for the model" and "AKIA is the prefix for AWS
access key ids" must survive verbatim, because a filter that mangles those
quietly corrupts normal memories forever.

Upstream restructuring since 47f281c changed where this lands. The original
patch hooked nine call sites; upstream has since funnelled every content
write through prepareStoredContent (AddObservation, both prompt paths and
UpdateObservation all call it), so content is now covered by one edit
instead of four. The remaining sites are the ones that bypass it: the two
title paths, the UpdateObservation empty-content guard, both SuggestTopicKey
derivations, and EndSession -- which upstream still persists with no
filtering of any kind, not even the tag strip.

preparePromptContent no longer exists; it is prepareStoredContent now and
returns TruncationMetadata alongside the text. Sanitizing inside it keeps
the byte accounting measuring what actually gets stored, which
TestContentTruncationMeasuresRedactedBytes covers upstream and still passes.

Full internal/store suite passes.

Consolidation (upstream #1782/#1825) writes its survivor through its own
INSERT in consolidate.go and never reaches prepareStoredContent. Its
caller-supplied title and content overrides are agent-authored text, so
they now go through sanitizeForStorage when the plan is built. That also
restores upstream's own <private> stripping on this path, which upstream
does not apply there either. An override that sanitizes to nothing falls
back to the source fields, which were filtered when they were stored.
TestConsolidateOverridesAreSanitizedBeforePersisting fails without it:
the raw credential already shows up in the plan returned to the caller.

